= Resistor — Technical =

== Electrical model ==
For an ideal ohmic two-terminal resistor under appropriate conditions:

<pre>
V = I R
P = V I = I^2 R = V^2 / R
</pre>

<code>V</code> is the potential difference in volts, <code>I</code> is conventional current in amperes, <code>R</code> is resistance in ohms, and <code>P</code> is dissipated power in watts. This is a '''component constitutive relation''', not an information-processing algorithm.

== Signal conditioning ==
In a voltage divider with ideal series resistors <code>R1</code> above <code>R2</code>, and an approximately unloaded output taken across <code>R2</code>:

<pre>
Vout = Vin * R2 / (R1 + R2)
</pre>

Real analog-to-digital converter inputs, leakage currents, source impedance and loading can invalidate that simple divider formula. Verify input bandwidth, voltage ratings, settling and reference uncertainty when using the divider for measurement.

A pull-up resistor establishes a bias for an otherwise undriven node. Actual digital interpretation requires the receiver's specified logic thresholds, leakage specifications, switching time and noise margin; a pull-up does not generate a logical value by abstract definition. A shunt resistor may allow current inference from a measured voltage difference, with power loss, thermal drift and measurement error accounted for.

== Physical limitations ==
Resistance tolerance, temperature coefficient, frequency-dependent parasitics and thermal noise limit the ideal model. The resistor can shape or bias a signal; the larger circuit performs sensing, state detection, conversion or computation.

